# Branches, Mistakes, and Figuring Out How Git Actually Works

Git has always been slightly intimidating to me. I understand the idea: branches keep your work organized and safe. But putting it into practice… let’s just say I learned some things the hard way.

I created a feature branch.  
Then accidentally created another feature branch with almost the same name.  
Then pushed the wrong one.  
Then realized I wasn’t following GitHub Flow properly at all.

## What Went Wrong

- I didn’t switch branches before committing  
- I forgot to delete old branches after merging  
- I pushed with the wrong refspec  
- I relied too much on VS Code’s UI and not enough on actually understanding the commands

## What I Learned

- Always check `git branch` before committing  
- Cleanup is part of the process  
- Naming conventions matter more than you think  
- Mistakes are normal — especially when learning version control

I used to think Git was just a technical requirement, but now I see it as a tool for thinking clearly about my work.  
